{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794780750184,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","part":{"id":"prt_1e144e168001576j6rL41ojm8E","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","messageID":"msg_1e144dab0001vtf8u3Yhi2Tl57","type":"step-start","snapshot":"2f13e5db2800c10a21c6959acdf5b8b7cccaeb82"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794780760930,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","part":{"partID":"prt_1e1450a67002DyiGo4RrZAwSsb","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","messageID":"msg_1e144dab0001vtf8u3Yhi2Tl57","type":"tool","id":"call_a87b16c9aef44025aaf5e8eb","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs"},"output":"Read file .skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs, lines 1-350\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Hook Kill-Switch Resolver\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \"use strict\";\n5: \n6: // Central kill-switch resolver for every repo-authored runtime hook. A hook is\n7: // enabled by default and only goes silent when the operator sets a kill-switch,\n8: // so adding this guard changes no behavior until a flag is set. The few concerns\n9: // in DEFAULT_OFF invert that and stay silent until their enable switch is set,\n10: // though a kill-switch still silences them. The master\n11: // switch disables the whole enforcement layer at once; a per-concern switch\n12: // disables one hook family across every runtime that carries it. Names that\n13: // predate this convention are honored as aliases so existing operator config\n14: // keeps working.\n15: //\n16: // Flags come from two places: the live environment, and an optional operator\n17: // config file (hook-flags.env) that lets a user persist their choices without\n18: // exporting env vars every session. The environment always wins over the file,\n19: // so a persistent default in the file can still be overridden per session.\n20: \n21: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n22: // 1. IMPORTS\n23: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n24: \n25: const fs = require(\"node:fs\");\n26: const path = require(\"node:path\");\n27: \n28: // Bridge legacy env names onto their new equivalents once, as early as possible.\n29: // Nearly every runtime hook loads this module, so wiring the bridge here covers\n30: // the whole library from a single place; entry points with no flag dependency\n31: // (the standalone daemon launchers) call it themselves.\n32: require(\"./env-aliases.cjs\").applyEnvAliases();\n33: \n34: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n35: // 2. CONSTANTS\n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: \n38: const MASTER_FLAG = \"SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED\";\n39: // The master switch was renamed from its opaque prefix to a self-describing one.\n40: // The old name stays honored so any operator who exported it keeps their opt-out.\n41: const MASTER_ALIASES = [\"MK_HOOKS_DISABLED\"];\n42: \n43: // Canonical per-concern kill-switch, where the new name does not follow the\n44: // default `SYSTEM_<CONCERN>_DISABLED` shape because the hook is owned by a named\n45: // skill or CLI surface rather than the framework core. Concerns not listed here\n46: // fall back to the default shape in concernFlag().\n47: const CONCERN_CANONICAL = {\n48:   goal: \"OPENCODE_GOAL_DISABLED\",\n49:   dispatch: \"CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\",\n50:   \"mcp-route-guard\": \"MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\",\n51:   \"codex-watchdog\": \"CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\",\n52:   \"git-preflight\": \"SK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\",\n53:   \"post-edit-quality\": \"SK_CODE_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\",\n54: };\n55: \n56: // concern -> extra env vars that also disable it. Three generations coexist here\n57: // so operator config written against any of them keeps working: the still-older\n58: // MK_/SPECKIT_ aliases, and the self-describing variant names that a concern's\n59: // own plugin/config/docs use where they differ from concernFlag()'s canonical\n60: // (e.g. a plugin's *_PLUGIN_DISABLED / *_HOOK_DISABLED const, or a doc's shorter\n61: // spelling). concernFlag() supplies the one canonical name; everything a real\n62: // surface also documents or exports for the same concern is listed here so the\n63: // documented switch actually disables the hook.\n64: const LEGACY_ALIASES = {\n65:   goal: [\"OPENCODE_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\"],\n66:   dispatch: [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\"],\n67:   // The preflight guard split off from the audit log, but the whole-surface\n68:   // switch that predates the split still has to silence both halves.\n69:   \"dispatch-guard\": [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n70:   \"skill-advisor\": [\n71:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n72:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n73:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED\",\n74:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n75:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n76:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n77:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n78:   ],\n79:   completion: [\n80:     \"SYSTEM_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n81:     \"SYSTEM_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n82:     \"MK_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n83:     \"MK_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n84:     \"MK_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n85:   ],\n86:   \"spec-gate\": [\"MK_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\", \"SPECKIT_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\"],\n87:   \"mcp-route-guard\": [\"MK_MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n88:   \"codex-watchdog\": [\"CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\"],\n89:   \"dist-freshness\": [\"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_DISABLED\", \"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n90:   \"git-preflight\": [\"MK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\"],\n91:   \"hook-install\": [\"MK_HOOK_INSTALL_DISABLED\"],\n92:   \"permission-policy\": [\"MK_PERMISSION_POLICY_DISABLED\"],\n93:   \"session-lifecycle\": [\"MK_SESSION_LIFECYCLE_DISABLED\"],\n94:   \"session-cleanup\": [\"MK_SESSION_CLEANUP_DISABLED\"],\n95:   \"task-dispatch\": [\"MK_TASK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n96:   \"post-edit-quality\": [\"MK_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\"],\n97: };\n98: \n99: // concern -> the switch that turns it on. A concern listed here is off until the\n100: // operator opts in, because it acts on the session itself (the relaunch loop can\n101: // end a running session and start a new one), except on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES\n102: // names for it. The opt-in resolves like every other flag, environment over file,\n103: // and the master and kill switches still win over it.\n104: const DEFAULT_OFF = {\n105:   \"session-handover\": \"SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED\",\n106: };\n107: \n108: // The CLI runtimes that can carry a default-off concern. An operator may want the\n109: // relaunch loop on in one runtime and off in another, so each runtime gets its own\n110: // enable switch, SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED, which answers before\n111: // the global one whenever it holds a value. A caller names its runtime explicitly;\n112: // the resolver never guesses it from the environment.\n113: const RUNTIMES = Object.freeze([\"claude\", \"codex\", \"pi\", \"opencode\", \"cursor\", \"devin\", \"vibe\"]);\n114: \n115: // concern -> the runtimes where a default-off concern is on when neither its runtime\n116: // switch nor its global switch holds a value. Claude Code's hand-over needs no extra\n117: // wiring beyond the hooks every install registers, so a project gets it without\n118: // finding the switch; the other runtimes still wait for their owner to opt in. A set\n119: // value, the master switch and the kill switches all answer before this default.\n120: const DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES = Object.freeze({\n121:   \"session-handover\": Object.freeze([\"claude\"]),\n122: });\n123: \n124: // Main switch prefix -> the second spelling also read for it. People call the hand-over\n125: // \"in-session handover\" too, so a switch written with that name works. The second\n126: // spelling answers only when the main switch is unset or blank, so the main name wins\n127: // whenever both hold a value.\n128: const SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES = Object.freeze({\n129:   SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_: \"SYSTEM_IN_SESSION_HANDOVER_\",\n130: });\n131: \n132: // The second spelling of a switch, or null when the switch has none.\n133: function switchAlias(key) {\n134:   for (const [prefix, aliasPrefix] of Object.entries(SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES)) {\n135:     if (key.startsWith(prefix)) return aliasPrefix + key.slice(prefix.length);\n136:   }\n137:   return null;\n138: }\n139: \n140: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n141: // 3. HELPERS\n142: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n143: \n144: // The operator config file: sibling of this shared/ dir, in the hooks hub root.\n145: // Overridable via HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG so tests and alternate layouts can point\n146: // elsewhere without touching the repo copy. Resolved per read (not at load) so\n147: // a late override is honored after _resetConfigCache().\n148: function configPath() {\n149:   return process.env.HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG || path.join(__dirname, \"..\", \"hook-flags.env\");\n150: }\n151: \n152: const EMPTY_CONFIG = Object.freeze({});\n153: \n154: function isTruthy(value) {\n155:   if (typeof value !== \"string\") return false;\n156:   const v = value.trim().toLowerCase();\n157:   return v === \"1\" || v === \"true\" || v === \"yes\" || v === \"on\";\n158: }\n159: \n160: // Parse a KEY=value config file into a plain object. Blank lines and lines\n161: // beginning with '#' are ignored; a '#' after a space or tab ends the value, so\n162: // the example file's lines, which carry a trailing comment, still work once\n163: // uncommented; surrounding single/double quotes on the value are stripped. The\n164: // other readers of this file (hook-flags.sh, sk-doc's validation_switch.py and\n165: // sk-code's check-dist-staleness.sh) parse it the same way. Any read/parse\n166: // failure returns {} so a broken or absent file can never disable a hook the\n167: // operator did not ask to disable (fail-open).\n168: function loadConfigFile(filePath) {\n169:   let raw;\n170:   try {\n171:     raw = fs.readFileSync(filePath, \"utf8\");\n172:   } catch {\n173:     return {};\n174:   }\n175:   const out = {};\n176:   for (const line of raw.split(/\\r?\\n/)) {\n177:     const trimmed = line.trim();\n178:     if (!trimmed || trimmed.startsWith(\"#\")) continue;\n179:     const eq = trimmed.indexOf(\"=\");\n180:     if (eq <= 0) continue;\n181:     const key = trimmed.slice(0, eq).trim();\n182:     if (!key) continue;\n183:     let val = trimmed.slice(eq + 1).replace(/[ \\t]#.*$/, \"\").trim();\n184:     if (\n185:       val.length >= 2 &&\n186:       ((val[0] === '\"' && val[val.length - 1] === '\"') ||\n187:         (val[0] === \"'\" && val[val.length - 1] === \"'\"))\n188:     ) {\n189:       val = val.slice(1, -1);\n190:     }\n191:     out[key] = val;\n192:   }\n193:   return out;\n194: }\n195: \n196: // The config file is read once per process — hooks run on every turn, so a\n197: // re-read per lookup would be wasteful. Tests reset this via _resetConfigCache.\n198: let cachedConfig = null;\n199: function fileConfig() {\n200:   if (cachedConfig === null) cachedConfig = loadConfigFile(configPath());\n201:   return cachedConfig;\n202: }\n203: function _resetConfigCache() {\n204:   cachedConfig = null;\n205: }\n206: \n207: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n208: // 4. CORE LOGIC\n209: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n210: \n211: // True unless the master switch or one of this concern's kill-switches is set.\n212: // A concern in DEFAULT_OFF also needs its enable switch set before it is true.\n213: // `env` is injectable for testing; it defaults to the live process environment.\n214: // When `env` is passed explicitly, the config file is skipped so tests stay\n215: // isolated — unless a `config` object is also injected to exercise the merge.\n216: // The environment always overrides the config file for a given key.\n217: // `options.runtime` names the calling runtime. For a default-off concern, that\n218: // runtime's enable switch decides whenever it resolves to a non-empty value, and\n219: // the global enable switch decides whenever it does. With both blank, a runtime in\n220: // DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES is on and any other is off. The master and kill switches still\n221: // win over all of them. With no runtime, or one outside RUNTIMES, only the global applies.\n222: function isHookEnabled(concern, env, config, options) {\n223:   return hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options).enabled;\n224: }\n225: \n226: // The single decision behind isHookEnabled, with the switch that made it, so a caller\n227: // can tell the operator which flag to change. `key` is the switch that decided, or null\n228: // when nothing did and the concern is on by default. `reason` says which rung of the\n229: // precedence answered: \"master\" (the master switch or an alias), \"kill\" (the concern's\n230: // kill switch or a legacy alias), \"runtime\" (the runtime's enable switch, which held a\n231: // value), \"global\" (the global enable switch, unset or set) or \"default\" (a default-on\n232: // concern, or a default-off one on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES names). Arguments are\n233: // those of isHookEnabled.\n234: function hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options) {\n235:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n236:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n237:   const cfg =\n238:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n239:       ? config\n240:       : hasEnv\n241:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n242:         : fileConfig();\n243:   const resolve = (key) => {\n244:     const fromEnv = source[key];\n245:     return fromEnv !== undefined ? fromEnv : cfg[key];\n246:   };\n247:   const holds = (value) => typeof value === \"string\" && value.trim() !== \"\";\n248:   // A switch's own value when it holds one, else its second spelling's when that holds\n249:   // one, so `key` names whichever switch the operator actually set.\n250:   const resolveSwitch = (key) => {\n251:     const value = resolve(key);\n252:     const alias = holds(value) ? null : switchAlias(key);\n253:     if (alias && holds(resolve(alias))) return { key: alias, value: resolve(alias) };\n254:     return { key, value };\n255:   };\n256:   for (const key of [MASTER_FLAG].concat(MASTER_ALIASES)) {\n257:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"master\" };\n258:   }\n259:   const flag = concernFlag(concern);\n260:   if (flag) {\n261:     const kill = resolveSwitch(flag);\n262:     if (isTruthy(kill.value)) return { enabled: false, key: kill.key, reason: \"kill\" };\n263:   }\n264:   for (const key of LEGACY_ALIASES[concern] || []) {\n265:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"kill\" };\n266:   }\n267:   if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) {\n268:     const runtimeFlag = runtimeEnableFlag(concern, options && options.runtime);\n269:     if (runtimeFlag) {\n270:       const runtimeSwitch = resolveSwitch(runtimeFlag);\n271:       if (holds(runtimeSwitch.value)) {\n272:         return { enabled: isTruthy(runtimeSwitch.value), key: runtimeSwitch.key, reason: \"runtime\" };\n273:       }\n274:     }\n275:     const globalSwitch = resolveSwitch(DEFAULT_OFF[concern]);\n276:     if (!holds(globalSwitch.value)) {\n277:       const runtime = options && typeof options.runtime === \"string\" ? options.runtime.trim().toLowerCase() : \"\";\n278:       if ((DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES[concern] || []).includes(runtime)) {\n279:         return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n280:       }\n281:     }\n282:     return { enabled: isTruthy(globalSwitch.value), key: globalSwitch.key, reason: \"global\" };\n283:   }\n284:   return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n285: }\n286: \n287: // The per-runtime enable switch for a default-off concern, such as\n288: // SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_CLAUDE_ENABLED, or null when the concern has no enable\n289: // switch or the runtime is not one of RUNTIMES.\n290: function runtimeEnableFlag(concern, runtime) {\n291:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) return null;\n292:   if (typeof runtime !== \"string\") return null;\n293:   const name = runtime.trim().toLowerCase();\n294:   if (!RUNTIMES.includes(name)) return null;\n295:   return DEFAULT_OFF[concern].replace(/_ENABLED$/, \"_\" + name.toUpperCase() + \"_ENABLED\");\n296: }\n297: \n298: // True when one named switch resolves truthy, for a switch that is not a hook\n299: // concern (a validator's opt-out, say) but is saved in the same file under the\n300: // same precedence: the environment answers whenever the name is set there, even\n301: // to an empty value, and the config file answers otherwise. `env` and `config`\n302: // are injectable exactly as they are for isHookEnabled.\n303: function isFlagOn(name, env, config) {\n304:   if (typeof name !== \"string\" || !name.trim()) return false;\n305:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n306:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n307:   if (source[name] !== undefined) return isTruthy(source[name]);\n308:   const cfg =\n309:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n310:       ? config\n311:       : hasEnv\n312:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n313:         : fileConfig();\n314:   return isTruthy(cfg[name]);\n315: }\n316: \n317: // The canonical per-concern kill-switch env var for a concern slug. A blank\n318: // concern has no flag (skipped), so callers never look up \"MK__DISABLED\".\n319: function concernFlag(concern) {\n320:   if (concern === null || concern === undefined) return null;\n321:   const slug = String(concern).trim();\n322:   if (!slug) return null;\n323:   if (CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug]) return CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug];\n324:   return \"SYSTEM_\" + slug.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9]+/g, \"_\") + \"_DISABLED\";\n325: }\n326: \n327: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n328: // 5. EXPORTS\n329: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n330: \n331: module.exports = {\n332:   isHookEnabled,\n333:   hookFlagDecision,\n334:   isFlagOn,\n335:   concernFlag,\n336:   runtimeEnableFlag,\n337:   isTruthy,\n338:   loadConfigFile,\n339:   MASTER_FLAG,\n340:   MASTER_ALIASES,\n341:   CONCERN_CANONICAL,\n342:   LEGACY_ALIASES,\n343:   DEFAULT_OFF,\n344:   DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES,\n345:   RUNTIMES,\n346:   SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES,\n347:   switchAlias,\n348:   configPath,\n349:   _resetConfigCache,\n350: };","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs, lines 1-350\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Hook Kill-Switch Resolver\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \"use strict\";\n5: \n6: // Central kill-switch resolver for every repo-authored runtime hook. A hook is\n7: // enabled by default and only goes silent when the operator sets a kill-switch,\n8: // so adding this guard changes no behavior until a flag is set. The few concerns\n9: // in DEFAULT_OFF invert that and stay silent until their enable switch is set,\n10: // though a kill-switch still silences them. The master\n11: // switch disables the whole enforcement layer at once; a per-concern switch\n12: // disables one hook family across every runtime that carries it. Names that\n13: // predate this convention are honored as aliases so existing operator config\n14: // keeps working.\n15: //\n16: // Flags come from two places: the live environment, and an optional operator\n17: // config file (hook-flags.env) that lets a user persist their choices without\n18: // exporting env vars every session. The environment always wins over the file,\n19: // so a persistent default in the file can still be overridden per session.\n20: \n21: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n22: // 1. IMPORTS\n23: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n24: \n25: const fs = require(\"node:fs\");\n26: const path = require(\"node:path\");\n27: \n28: // Bridge legacy env names onto their new equivalents once, as early as possible.\n29: // Nearly every runtime hook loads this module, so wiring the bridge here covers\n30: // the whole library from a single place; entry points with no flag dependency\n31: // (the standalone daemon launchers) call it themselves.\n32: require(\"./env-aliases.cjs\").applyEnvAliases();\n33: \n34: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n35: // 2. CONSTANTS\n36: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n37: \n38: const MASTER_FLAG = \"SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED\";\n39: // The master switch was renamed from its opaque prefix to a self-describing one.\n40: // The old name stays honored so any operator who exported it keeps their opt-out.\n41: const MASTER_ALIASES = [\"MK_HOOKS_DISABLED\"];\n42: \n43: // Canonical per-concern kill-switch, where the new name does not follow the\n44: // default `SYSTEM_<CONCERN>_DISABLED` shape because the hook is owned by a named\n45: // skill or CLI surface rather than the framework core. Concerns not listed here\n46: // fall back to the default shape in concernFlag().\n47: const CONCERN_CANONICAL = {\n48:   goal: \"OPENCODE_GOAL_DISABLED\",\n49:   dispatch: \"CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\",\n50:   \"mcp-route-guard\": \"MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\",\n51:   \"codex-watchdog\": \"CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\",\n52:   \"git-preflight\": \"SK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\",\n53:   \"post-edit-quality\": \"SK_CODE_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\",\n54: };\n55: \n56: // concern -> extra env vars that also disable it. Three generations coexist here\n57: // so operator config written against any of them keeps working: the still-older\n58: // MK_/SPECKIT_ aliases, and the self-describing variant names that a concern's\n59: // own plugin/config/docs use where they differ from concernFlag()'s canonical\n60: // (e.g. a plugin's *_PLUGIN_DISABLED / *_HOOK_DISABLED const, or a doc's shorter\n61: // spelling). concernFlag() supplies the one canonical name; everything a real\n62: // surface also documents or exports for the same concern is listed here so the\n63: // documented switch actually disables the hook.\n64: const LEGACY_ALIASES = {\n65:   goal: [\"OPENCODE_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_DISABLED\", \"MK_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED\"],\n66:   dispatch: [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED\"],\n67:   // The preflight guard split off from the audit log, but the whole-surface\n68:   // switch that predates the split still has to silence both halves.\n69:   \"dispatch-guard\": [\"SYSTEM_DISPATCH_DISABLED\", \"MK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n70:   \"skill-advisor\": [\n71:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n72:     \"SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n73:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED\",\n74:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n75:     \"MK_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n76:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED\",\n77:     \"SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED\",\n78:   ],\n79:   completion: [\n80:     \"SYSTEM_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n81:     \"SYSTEM_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n82:     \"MK_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n83:     \"MK_COMPLETION_SENTINEL_DISABLED\",\n84:     \"MK_SPECKIT_COMPLETION_DISABLED\",\n85:   ],\n86:   \"spec-gate\": [\"MK_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\", \"SPECKIT_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED\"],\n87:   \"mcp-route-guard\": [\"MK_MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n88:   \"codex-watchdog\": [\"CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\", \"MK_CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED\"],\n89:   \"dist-freshness\": [\"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_DISABLED\", \"MK_DIST_FRESHNESS_GUARD_DISABLED\"],\n90:   \"git-preflight\": [\"MK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED\"],\n91:   \"hook-install\": [\"MK_HOOK_INSTALL_DISABLED\"],\n92:   \"permission-policy\": [\"MK_PERMISSION_POLICY_DISABLED\"],\n93:   \"session-lifecycle\": [\"MK_SESSION_LIFECYCLE_DISABLED\"],\n94:   \"session-cleanup\": [\"MK_SESSION_CLEANUP_DISABLED\"],\n95:   \"task-dispatch\": [\"MK_TASK_DISPATCH_DISABLED\"],\n96:   \"post-edit-quality\": [\"MK_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED\"],\n97: };\n98: \n99: // concern -> the switch that turns it on. A concern listed here is off until the\n100: // operator opts in, because it acts on the session itself (the relaunch loop can\n101: // end a running session and start a new one), except on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES\n102: // names for it. The opt-in resolves like every other flag, environment over file,\n103: // and the master and kill switches still win over it.\n104: const DEFAULT_OFF = {\n105:   \"session-handover\": \"SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED\",\n106: };\n107: \n108: // The CLI runtimes that can carry a default-off concern. An operator may want the\n109: // relaunch loop on in one runtime and off in another, so each runtime gets its own\n110: // enable switch, SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED, which answers before\n111: // the global one whenever it holds a value. A caller names its runtime explicitly;\n112: // the resolver never guesses it from the environment.\n113: const RUNTIMES = Object.freeze([\"claude\", \"codex\", \"pi\", \"opencode\", \"cursor\", \"devin\", \"vibe\"]);\n114: \n115: // concern -> the runtimes where a default-off concern is on when neither its runtime\n116: // switch nor its global switch holds a value. Claude Code's hand-over needs no extra\n117: // wiring beyond the hooks every install registers, so a project gets it without\n118: // finding the switch; the other runtimes still wait for their owner to opt in. A set\n119: // value, the master switch and the kill switches all answer before this default.\n120: const DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES = Object.freeze({\n121:   \"session-handover\": Object.freeze([\"claude\"]),\n122: });\n123: \n124: // Main switch prefix -> the second spelling also read for it. People call the hand-over\n125: // \"in-session handover\" too, so a switch written with that name works. The second\n126: // spelling answers only when the main switch is unset or blank, so the main name wins\n127: // whenever both hold a value.\n128: const SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES = Object.freeze({\n129:   SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_: \"SYSTEM_IN_SESSION_HANDOVER_\",\n130: });\n131: \n132: // The second spelling of a switch, or null when the switch has none.\n133: function switchAlias(key) {\n134:   for (const [prefix, aliasPrefix] of Object.entries(SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES)) {\n135:     if (key.startsWith(prefix)) return aliasPrefix + key.slice(prefix.length);\n136:   }\n137:   return null;\n138: }\n139: \n140: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n141: // 3. HELPERS\n142: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n143: \n144: // The operator config file: sibling of this shared/ dir, in the hooks hub root.\n145: // Overridable via HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG so tests and alternate layouts can point\n146: // elsewhere without touching the repo copy. Resolved per read (not at load) so\n147: // a late override is honored after _resetConfigCache().\n148: function configPath() {\n149:   return process.env.HOOK_FLAGS_CONFIG || path.join(__dirname, \"..\", \"hook-flags.env\");\n150: }\n151: \n152: const EMPTY_CONFIG = Object.freeze({});\n153: \n154: function isTruthy(value) {\n155:   if (typeof value !== \"string\") return false;\n156:   const v = value.trim().toLowerCase();\n157:   return v === \"1\" || v === \"true\" || v === \"yes\" || v === \"on\";\n158: }\n159: \n160: // Parse a KEY=value config file into a plain object. Blank lines and lines\n161: // beginning with '#' are ignored; a '#' after a space or tab ends the value, so\n162: // the example file's lines, which carry a trailing comment, still work once\n163: // uncommented; surrounding single/double quotes on the value are stripped. The\n164: // other readers of this file (hook-flags.sh, sk-doc's validation_switch.py and\n165: // sk-code's check-dist-staleness.sh) parse it the same way. Any read/parse\n166: // failure returns {} so a broken or absent file can never disable a hook the\n167: // operator did not ask to disable (fail-open).\n168: function loadConfigFile(filePath) {\n169:   let raw;\n170:   try {\n171:     raw = fs.readFileSync(filePath, \"utf8\");\n172:   } catch {\n173:     return {};\n174:   }\n175:   const out = {};\n176:   for (const line of raw.split(/\\r?\\n/)) {\n177:     const trimmed = line.trim();\n178:     if (!trimmed || trimmed.startsWith(\"#\")) continue;\n179:     const eq = trimmed.indexOf(\"=\");\n180:     if (eq <= 0) continue;\n181:     const key = trimmed.slice(0, eq).trim();\n182:     if (!key) continue;\n183:     let val = trimmed.slice(eq + 1).replace(/[ \\t]#.*$/, \"\").trim();\n184:     if (\n185:       val.length >= 2 &&\n186:       ((val[0] === '\"' && val[val.length - 1] === '\"') ||\n187:         (val[0] === \"'\" && val[val.length - 1] === \"'\"))\n188:     ) {\n189:       val = val.slice(1, -1);\n190:     }\n191:     out[key] = val;\n192:   }\n193:   return out;\n194: }\n195: \n196: // The config file is read once per process — hooks run on every turn, so a\n197: // re-read per lookup would be wasteful. Tests reset this via _resetConfigCache.\n198: let cachedConfig = null;\n199: function fileConfig() {\n200:   if (cachedConfig === null) cachedConfig = loadConfigFile(configPath());\n201:   return cachedConfig;\n202: }\n203: function _resetConfigCache() {\n204:   cachedConfig = null;\n205: }\n206: \n207: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n208: // 4. CORE LOGIC\n209: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n210: \n211: // True unless the master switch or one of this concern's kill-switches is set.\n212: // A concern in DEFAULT_OFF also needs its enable switch set before it is true.\n213: // `env` is injectable for testing; it defaults to the live process environment.\n214: // When `env` is passed explicitly, the config file is skipped so tests stay\n215: // isolated — unless a `config` object is also injected to exercise the merge.\n216: // The environment always overrides the config file for a given key.\n217: // `options.runtime` names the calling runtime. For a default-off concern, that\n218: // runtime's enable switch decides whenever it resolves to a non-empty value, and\n219: // the global enable switch decides whenever it does. With both blank, a runtime in\n220: // DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES is on and any other is off. The master and kill switches still\n221: // win over all of them. With no runtime, or one outside RUNTIMES, only the global applies.\n222: function isHookEnabled(concern, env, config, options) {\n223:   return hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options).enabled;\n224: }\n225: \n226: // The single decision behind isHookEnabled, with the switch that made it, so a caller\n227: // can tell the operator which flag to change. `key` is the switch that decided, or null\n228: // when nothing did and the concern is on by default. `reason` says which rung of the\n229: // precedence answered: \"master\" (the master switch or an alias), \"kill\" (the concern's\n230: // kill switch or a legacy alias), \"runtime\" (the runtime's enable switch, which held a\n231: // value), \"global\" (the global enable switch, unset or set) or \"default\" (a default-on\n232: // concern, or a default-off one on a runtime DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES names). Arguments are\n233: // those of isHookEnabled.\n234: function hookFlagDecision(concern, env, config, options) {\n235:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n236:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n237:   const cfg =\n238:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n239:       ? config\n240:       : hasEnv\n241:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n242:         : fileConfig();\n243:   const resolve = (key) => {\n244:     const fromEnv = source[key];\n245:     return fromEnv !== undefined ? fromEnv : cfg[key];\n246:   };\n247:   const holds = (value) => typeof value === \"string\" && value.trim() !== \"\";\n248:   // A switch's own value when it holds one, else its second spelling's when that holds\n249:   // one, so `key` names whichever switch the operator actually set.\n250:   const resolveSwitch = (key) => {\n251:     const value = resolve(key);\n252:     const alias = holds(value) ? null : switchAlias(key);\n253:     if (alias && holds(resolve(alias))) return { key: alias, value: resolve(alias) };\n254:     return { key, value };\n255:   };\n256:   for (const key of [MASTER_FLAG].concat(MASTER_ALIASES)) {\n257:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"master\" };\n258:   }\n259:   const flag = concernFlag(concern);\n260:   if (flag) {\n261:     const kill = resolveSwitch(flag);\n262:     if (isTruthy(kill.value)) return { enabled: false, key: kill.key, reason: \"kill\" };\n263:   }\n264:   for (const key of LEGACY_ALIASES[concern] || []) {\n265:     if (isTruthy(resolve(key))) return { enabled: false, key, reason: \"kill\" };\n266:   }\n267:   if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) {\n268:     const runtimeFlag = runtimeEnableFlag(concern, options && options.runtime);\n269:     if (runtimeFlag) {\n270:       const runtimeSwitch = resolveSwitch(runtimeFlag);\n271:       if (holds(runtimeSwitch.value)) {\n272:         return { enabled: isTruthy(runtimeSwitch.value), key: runtimeSwitch.key, reason: \"runtime\" };\n273:       }\n274:     }\n275:     const globalSwitch = resolveSwitch(DEFAULT_OFF[concern]);\n276:     if (!holds(globalSwitch.value)) {\n277:       const runtime = options && typeof options.runtime === \"string\" ? options.runtime.trim().toLowerCase() : \"\";\n278:       if ((DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES[concern] || []).includes(runtime)) {\n279:         return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n280:       }\n281:     }\n282:     return { enabled: isTruthy(globalSwitch.value), key: globalSwitch.key, reason: \"global\" };\n283:   }\n284:   return { enabled: true, key: null, reason: \"default\" };\n285: }\n286: \n287: // The per-runtime enable switch for a default-off concern, such as\n288: // SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_CLAUDE_ENABLED, or null when the concern has no enable\n289: // switch or the runtime is not one of RUNTIMES.\n290: function runtimeEnableFlag(concern, runtime) {\n291:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(DEFAULT_OFF, concern)) return null;\n292:   if (typeof runtime !== \"string\") return null;\n293:   const name = runtime.trim().toLowerCase();\n294:   if (!RUNTIMES.includes(name)) return null;\n295:   return DEFAULT_OFF[concern].replace(/_ENABLED$/, \"_\" + name.toUpperCase() + \"_ENABLED\");\n296: }\n297: \n298: // True when one named switch resolves truthy, for a switch that is not a hook\n299: // concern (a validator's opt-out, say) but is saved in the same file under the\n300: // same precedence: the environment answers whenever the name is set there, even\n301: // to an empty value, and the config file answers otherwise. `env` and `config`\n302: // are injectable exactly as they are for isHookEnabled.\n303: function isFlagOn(name, env, config) {\n304:   if (typeof name !== \"string\" || !name.trim()) return false;\n305:   const hasEnv = env !== undefined && env !== null;\n306:   const source = hasEnv ? env : process.env;\n307:   if (source[name] !== undefined) return isTruthy(source[name]);\n308:   const cfg =\n309:     config !== undefined && config !== null\n310:       ? config\n311:       : hasEnv\n312:         ? EMPTY_CONFIG\n313:         : fileConfig();\n314:   return isTruthy(cfg[name]);\n315: }\n316: \n317: // The canonical per-concern kill-switch env var for a concern slug. A blank\n318: // concern has no flag (skipped), so callers never look up \"MK__DISABLED\".\n319: function concernFlag(concern) {\n320:   if (concern === null || concern === undefined) return null;\n321:   const slug = String(concern).trim();\n322:   if (!slug) return null;\n323:   if (CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug]) return CONCERN_CANONICAL[slug];\n324:   return \"SYSTEM_\" + slug.toUpperCase().replace(/[^A-Z0-9]+/g, \"_\") + \"_DISABLED\";\n325: }\n326: \n327: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n328: // 5. EXPORTS\n329: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n330: \n331: module.exports = {\n332:   isHookEnabled,\n333:   hookFlagDecision,\n334:   isFlagOn,\n335:   concernFlag,\n336:   runtimeEnableFlag,\n337:   isTruthy,\n338:   loadConfigFile,\n339:   MASTER_FLAG,\n340:   MASTER_ALIASES,\n341:   CONCERN_CANONICAL,\n342:   LEGACY_ALIASES,\n343:   DEFAULT_OFF,\n344:   DEFAULT_ON_RUNTIMES,\n345:   RUNTIMES,\n346:   SWITCH_ALIAS_PREFIXES,\n347:   switchAlias,\n348:   configPath,\n349:   _resetConfigCache,\n350: };"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794780760679,"end":1794780760930}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794780762471,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","part":{"id":"prt_1e1451167001V6T9zbb1ImK9a8","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","messageID":"msg_1e144dab0001vtf8u3Yhi2Tl57","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"2f13e5db2800c10a21c6959acdf5b8b7cccaeb82","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1054,"output":50,"reasoning":1021,"cache":{"read":76288,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794780765533,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","part":{"id":"prt_1e1451d5d001AIqftBsKfRthFg","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","messageID":"msg_1e145116c001vjtQj2D80kcnFW","type":"step-start","snapshot":"2f13e5db2800c10a21c6959acdf5b8b7cccaeb82"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794780775681,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","part":{"partID":"prt_1e14540c0002rbbwRn0FHWghJU","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","messageID":"msg_1e145116c001vjtQj2D80kcnFW","type":"tool","id":"call_273ccb9c6a54440f85730b73","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md","offset":60,"limit":120},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md, lines 60-179\n60: \n61: The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n62: \n63: A hub whose compiled route defers or asks to clarify, and whose modes have commands, renders as `<hub> (needs a mode)` with no command, for example:\n64: \n65: ```text\n66: Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.\n67: ```\n68: \n69: The recommendation carries `needsMode: true`. A command on the same recommendation wins and the label is dropped.\n70: \n71: ### Degraded contract\n72: \n73: When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n74: \n75: - A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n76: - Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n77: \n78: Rows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. PER-RUNTIME DELIVERY\n83: \n84: Every native runtime, Pi, DSH and Vibe run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`. The OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. The native adapters differ in prompt event, transport and delivery channel.\n85: \n86: | Runtime | Adapter | Event / wiring | Payload difference it handles | Delivery |\n87: |---|---|---|---|---|\n88: | **Claude** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `system-skill-advisor/.../dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.claude/settings.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 3s | Shim resolves the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor (install-anchored, not CWD-relative). Bounded stdin (1 MiB), bounded child stdio, 2.5s child timeout, `SIGKILL` on timeout | `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON on stdout. `{}` on any failure |\n89: | **Codex** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.codex/hooks.json`, timeout 3s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${CODEX_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope on resolution failure | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n90: | **Cursor** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js` | `beforeSubmitPrompt` in `.cursor/hooks.json`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n91: | **Devin** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${DEVIN_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n92: | **Pi** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` (real file, re-exported by its one-line `.pi/extensions/` entry) | `user_input` / prompt event via `.pi/extensions/` | Imports the advisor module **in-process** (not a subprocess). Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send. In-process calls remove the stall and let the module-level prompt cache work. Captures raw user text (bounded 32 KiB, 64-session LRU). The in-process call passes `runtime: 'pi'` and races a timer of the advisor budget plus 300 ms, delivering the fallback directives if the call hangs | Model-visible message. Delivery-state machine suppresses same-session re-delivery |\n93: | **OpenCode** | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | Plugin, loaded by OpenCode's flat glob over `.opencode/plugins/`. `experimental.chat.system.transform` per prompt, `event` for session lifecycle, `tool` for status | Shells out to the advisor CLI (bounded timeout, stdout cap 256 KiB, grace termination), TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, transform-dedup integration | Brief appended to `output.system`. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` tool returns a key=value report (no raw path leakage) |\n94: | **DSH** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs` (real file, reached through the `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/prompt-advisor.mjs` symlink) | `agent/pre-step` through the `repo-guards` plugin, on unless the `promptAdvisor` row flag is `false` | Proxies the latest operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `dsh`. A fan-out leaf, marked by the lineage patch, gets no brief | Brief added to the step |\n95: | **Vibe** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/vibe/prompt-advisor.mjs`, called by `.skilled/hooks/session-lifecycle/vibe/turn-start.mjs` | `pre_agent` in `.vibe/hooks.toml` (`turn-start`, match `main`), rendered into a dispatch's `VIBE_HOME` | Proxies the operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `vibe`. A child dispatch and a subagent turn get no brief | `hook_specific_output.additional_context`; the routing head and directives repeat only when they change, because Vibe keeps added context in the conversation. A head that names a command gains one line naming each command's document under `.skilled/commands/`, because Vibe has no command loader and its prompt stubs name the dash form |\n96: \n97: OpenCode has no source-hook adapter in `system-spec-kit`. Its prompt-time integration is the committed plugin, which shells out to the advisor CLI and inherits the shared render contract. The hooks-tree index at `.skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/` mirrors the per-runtime shims and the OpenCode plugin via relative symlinks. This README is the symlink target.\n98: \n99: ### Starting a surfaced command\n100: \n101: The document behind a surfaced command is `.skilled/commands/<group>/<name>.md`. The model reads that document in full and follows it, on every runtime. It never tells the user to type a slash command, and it asks the user to type one only when it cannot read the file.\n102: \n103: | Runtime | Model can start a command itself | Basis |\n104: |---|---|---|\n105: | Claude Code | Yes, through the Skill tool, as a shortcut for the names it lists | The Skill tool lists names, not files. A command file named `skill.md` in any capitalisation makes Claude Code read its folder as one skill, an open upstream bug, so no command file carries that name |\n106: | Codex | No | The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n107: | Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Cursor docs were not fetched |\n108: | Devin | No command surface at all | Skills are exposed, commands are not |\n109: | Pi | No model path found | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Pi docs were not fetched, so this is unverified beyond the repository |\n110: | OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | Commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n111: | DSH | No | dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader |\n112: | Vibe | No | Vibe has no command loader. The brief names each offered command's document, which the model reads in full |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. DIRECTORY TREE\n117: \n118: ```text\n119: system-skill-advisor/hooks/\n120: +-- skill-advisor-hook.md            # this reference (symlinked from .skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/README.md)\n121: +-- skill-advisor-hook-validation.md # validation/audit detail\n122: +-- claude/\n123: |   +-- README.md\n124: |   +-- directive-lifecycle-boundary.ts\n125: |   `-- user-prompt-submit.ts         # real advisor handler (Claude shape)\n126: +-- dsh/\n127: |   +-- README.md\n128: |   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real DSH adapter (.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/ imports it)\n129: +-- pi/\n130: |   +-- README.md\n131: |   `-- prompt-advisor.ts             # real Pi extension (.pi/extensions/ re-exports it)\n132: +-- vibe/\n133: |   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real Vibe part (the turn-start composer calls it)\n134: `-- lib/\n135:     +-- README.md\n136:     +-- directive-lifecycle.ts, directive-lifecycle-contract.ts, directive-lifecycle-file-store.ts\n137:     +-- directive-lifecycle-store.py, directive-lifecycle-vectors.json\n138:     `-- skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts\n139: \n140: system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.{ts,js}  # thin shims (claude/codex/cursor/devin)\n141: system-skill-advisor/runtime/\n142: +-- dist/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.js   # compiled advisor the shims spawn\n143: +-- lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts, render.ts, metrics.ts, subprocess.ts\n144: `-- scripts/skill_advisor.py                      # standalone Python advisor CLI\n145: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js         # OpenCode plugin\n146: ```\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. KEY FILES\n151: \n152: | File | Responsibility |\n153: |---|---|\n154: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` | The real advisor handler (Claude shape). Builds the brief, runs the CLI fallback, renders, applies directive-lifecycle dedup, emits the `additionalContext` envelope, persists a diagnostic record. The codex/cursor/devin siblings share the same advisor core over their runtime's payload shape. |\n155: | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.ts` | Thin process-boundary shims. Resolve the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor, spawn the compiled advisor with bounded stdin/stdio/timeout, and relay its stdout (or `{}` on any failure). |\n156: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` | Native Pi extension. Imports the advisor module in-process, captures raw user text (bounded LRU), runs a delivery-state machine that suppresses same-session re-delivery, and sends the brief as a model-visible message. Races the advisor call against a deadline of the advisor budget plus 300 ms and delivers the fallback directives if it hangs. |\n157: | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | OpenCode plugin. `experimental.chat.system.transform` spawns the bridge and appends the brief to `output.system`. `event` manages session lifecycle and cache invalidation. `tool: spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` returns a sanitized status report. TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup and transform-dedup integration. |\n158: | `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs` | The CLI front door the OpenCode plugin spawns. Receives the `advisor_recommend` command line, returns the JSON payload on stdout. |\n159: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts` | The brief builder: scoring, freshness, status, confidence/uncertainty. |\n160: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | `renderAdvisorBrief` / `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`, produces the model-visible text. |\n161: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/directive-lifecycle.ts` | Directive-lifecycle dedup: decides whether a directive should be re-delivered to a session within its cadence. |\n162: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` | `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli` is the hook's CLI caller. It runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` with the hook timeout and sends the request on stdin. The CLI owns daemon startup and local-scorer fallback. The function resolves the CLI, IPC bridge and database paths from one root. It searches `.skilled` first and `.opencode` second. |\n163: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Standalone Python advisor CLI. The advisor CLI runs it as the local scorer when the daemon is unreachable. The hook never calls it directly. |\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 6. CONFIGURATION\n168: \n169: The advisor is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes`, and `on` (case-insensitive) for the shared resolver.\n170: \n171: | Variable | Effect |\n172: |---|---|\n173: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED=1` | Canonical kill-switch. Every adapter checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` and returns empty/skipped prompt-safe output, and the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstains. |\n174: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. |\n175: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. |\n176: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias that disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. The standalone Python CLI (`skill_advisor.py`) reads only this name, set to exactly `1`, from `runtime/schemas/compat-contract.json`. |\n177: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias. Disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. |\n178: | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n179: | `MK_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Older alias of `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`. Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 180]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md, lines 60-179\n60: \n61: The ambiguous head applies the same label to both sides: `Advisor: <freshness>; ambiguous: <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> vs <label> <confidence>/<uncertainty> pass.` Each label part passes `sanitizeSkillLabel`, so a control character, a line break or an instruction-shaped label renders nothing rather than reaching the model. The `Also:` line still names a doctor command by its runnable string.\n62: \n63: A hub whose compiled route defers or asks to clarify, and whose modes have commands, renders as `<hub> (needs a mode)` with no command, for example:\n64: \n65: ```text\n66: Advisor: live; use system-deep-loop (needs a mode) 0.82/0.20 pass.\n67: ```\n68: \n69: The recommendation carries `needsMode: true`. A command on the same recommendation wins and the label is dropped.\n70: \n71: ### Degraded contract\n72: \n73: When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI runs the local Python scorer and returns `status: ok` with `degraded: true` and `source: local-scorer`. The local scorer returns command row ids, which a reader cannot type, so the CLI rewrites each row before the brief renders:\n74: \n75: - A row whose bare slash command appears in the prompt (the row's `command` text before the first space) keeps its command fields: `skillId` is the row id, `kind` is `command`, `command` is the runnable string, `policy` is present when the row has one and `ownerSkillId` names the owning skill. A typed `/speckit:save this session` renders `system-spec-kit → /speckit:save (start)`.\n76: - Every other row collapses to its owner skill: `skillId` is `ownerSkillId` and `kind` is `skill`. It is dropped when that skill is already offered, so the degraded brief never repeats a skill.\n77: \n78: Rows below the threshold are discarded before either rule applies. The degraded brief therefore keeps the same head form and names a skill that exists rather than a raw row id.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. PER-RUNTIME DELIVERY\n83: \n84: Every native runtime, Pi, DSH and Vibe run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`. The OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. The native adapters differ in prompt event, transport and delivery channel.\n85: \n86: | Runtime | Adapter | Event / wiring | Payload difference it handles | Delivery |\n87: |---|---|---|---|---|\n88: | **Claude** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `system-skill-advisor/.../dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.claude/settings.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 3s | Shim resolves the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor (install-anchored, not CWD-relative). Bounded stdin (1 MiB), bounded child stdio, 2.5s child timeout, `SIGKILL` on timeout | `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` JSON on stdout. `{}` on any failure |\n89: | **Codex** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.codex/hooks.json`, timeout 3s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${CODEX_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope on resolution failure | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n90: | **Cursor** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/cursor/user-prompt-submit.js` | `beforeSubmitPrompt` in `.cursor/hooks.json`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n91: | **Devin** | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.ts` (shim) → `.../dist/hooks/devin/user-prompt-submit.js` | `UserPromptSubmit` in `.devin/hooks.v1.json`, matcher `\"\"`, timeout 10s | Same shim dispatch. `cd \"${DEVIN_PROJECT_DIR:-$PWD}\"`. Fallback `printf` envelope | Same `additionalContext` JSON |\n92: | **Pi** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` (real file, re-exported by its one-line `.pi/extensions/` entry) | `user_input` / prompt event via `.pi/extensions/` | Imports the advisor module **in-process** (not a subprocess). Pi awaits input handlers before agent processing, so the old blocking-spawn bridge stalled every send. In-process calls remove the stall and let the module-level prompt cache work. Captures raw user text (bounded 32 KiB, 64-session LRU). The in-process call passes `runtime: 'pi'` and races a timer of the advisor budget plus 300 ms, delivering the fallback directives if the call hangs | Model-visible message. Delivery-state machine suppresses same-session re-delivery |\n93: | **OpenCode** | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | Plugin, loaded by OpenCode's flat glob over `.opencode/plugins/`. `experimental.chat.system.transform` per prompt, `event` for session lifecycle, `tool` for status | Shells out to the advisor CLI (bounded timeout, stdout cap 256 KiB, grace termination), TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, transform-dedup integration | Brief appended to `output.system`. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` tool returns a key=value report (no raw path leakage) |\n94: | **DSH** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/dsh/prompt-advisor.mjs` (real file, reached through the `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/prompt-advisor.mjs` symlink) | `agent/pre-step` through the `repo-guards` plugin, on unless the `promptAdvisor` row flag is `false` | Proxies the latest operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `dsh`. A fan-out leaf, marked by the lineage patch, gets no brief | Brief added to the step |\n95: | **Vibe** | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/vibe/prompt-advisor.mjs`, called by `.skilled/hooks/session-lifecycle/vibe/turn-start.mjs` | `pre_agent` in `.vibe/hooks.toml` (`turn-start`, match `main`), rendered into a dispatch's `VIBE_HOME` | Proxies the operator prompt through the compiled Claude advisor chain under the runtime label `vibe`. A child dispatch and a subagent turn get no brief | `hook_specific_output.additional_context`; the routing head and directives repeat only when they change, because Vibe keeps added context in the conversation. A head that names a command gains one line naming each command's document under `.skilled/commands/`, because Vibe has no command loader and its prompt stubs name the dash form |\n96: \n97: OpenCode has no source-hook adapter in `system-spec-kit`. Its prompt-time integration is the committed plugin, which shells out to the advisor CLI and inherits the shared render contract. The hooks-tree index at `.skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/` mirrors the per-runtime shims and the OpenCode plugin via relative symlinks. This README is the symlink target.\n98: \n99: ### Starting a surfaced command\n100: \n101: The document behind a surfaced command is `.skilled/commands/<group>/<name>.md`. The model reads that document in full and follows it, on every runtime. It never tells the user to type a slash command, and it asks the user to type one only when it cannot read the file.\n102: \n103: | Runtime | Model can start a command itself | Basis |\n104: |---|---|---|\n105: | Claude Code | Yes, through the Skill tool, as a shortcut for the names it lists | The Skill tool lists names, not files. A command file named `skill.md` in any capitalisation makes Claude Code read its folder as one skill, an open upstream bug, so no command file carries that name |\n106: | Codex | No | The mirror is a pointer stub that tells the model to read the command file. There is no `.codex/skills` |\n107: | Cursor | No model path found, and the brief is dormant | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Cursor docs were not fetched |\n108: | Devin | No command surface at all | Skills are exposed, commands are not |\n109: | Pi | No model path found | The mirror is a pointer stub. Public Pi docs were not fetched, so this is unverified beyond the repository |\n110: | OpenCode | No documented model path. A parent process can start one with `opencode run --command` | Commands are user-triggered templates, while skills are loaded by the model through a skill tool |\n111: | DSH | No | dsh has no agent-file loader and no command loader |\n112: | Vibe | No | Vibe has no command loader. The brief names each offered command's document, which the model reads in full |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. DIRECTORY TREE\n117: \n118: ```text\n119: system-skill-advisor/hooks/\n120: +-- skill-advisor-hook.md            # this reference (symlinked from .skilled/hooks/skill-advisor/README.md)\n121: +-- skill-advisor-hook-validation.md # validation/audit detail\n122: +-- claude/\n123: |   +-- README.md\n124: |   +-- directive-lifecycle-boundary.ts\n125: |   `-- user-prompt-submit.ts         # real advisor handler (Claude shape)\n126: +-- dsh/\n127: |   +-- README.md\n128: |   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real DSH adapter (.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/adapters/ imports it)\n129: +-- pi/\n130: |   +-- README.md\n131: |   `-- prompt-advisor.ts             # real Pi extension (.pi/extensions/ re-exports it)\n132: +-- vibe/\n133: |   `-- prompt-advisor.mjs            # real Vibe part (the turn-start composer calls it)\n134: `-- lib/\n135:     +-- README.md\n136:     +-- directive-lifecycle.ts, directive-lifecycle-contract.ts, directive-lifecycle-file-store.ts\n137:     +-- directive-lifecycle-store.py, directive-lifecycle-vectors.json\n138:     `-- skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts\n139: \n140: system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.{ts,js}  # thin shims (claude/codex/cursor/devin)\n141: system-skill-advisor/runtime/\n142: +-- dist/hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.js   # compiled advisor the shims spawn\n143: +-- lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts, render.ts, metrics.ts, subprocess.ts\n144: `-- scripts/skill_advisor.py                      # standalone Python advisor CLI\n145: .opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js         # OpenCode plugin\n146: ```\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. KEY FILES\n151: \n152: | File | Responsibility |\n153: |---|---|\n154: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.ts` | The real advisor handler (Claude shape). Builds the brief, runs the CLI fallback, renders, applies directive-lifecycle dedup, emits the `additionalContext` envelope, persists a diagnostic record. The codex/cursor/devin siblings share the same advisor core over their runtime's payload shape. |\n155: | `system-spec-kit/.../hooks/<runtime>/user-prompt-submit.ts` | Thin process-boundary shims. Resolve the advisor target by walking up to the `.opencode` ancestor, spawn the compiled advisor with bounded stdin/stdio/timeout, and relay its stdout (or `{}` on any failure). |\n156: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/pi/prompt-advisor.ts` | Native Pi extension. Imports the advisor module in-process, captures raw user text (bounded LRU), runs a delivery-state machine that suppresses same-session re-delivery, and sends the brief as a model-visible message. Races the advisor call against a deadline of the advisor budget plus 300 ms and delivers the fallback directives if it hangs. |\n157: | `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js` | OpenCode plugin. `experimental.chat.system.transform` spawns the bridge and appends the brief to `output.system`. `event` manages session lifecycle and cache invalidation. `tool: spec_kit_skill_advisor_status` returns a sanitized status report. TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup and transform-dedup integration. |\n158: | `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs` | The CLI front door the OpenCode plugin spawns. Receives the `advisor_recommend` command line, returns the JSON payload on stdout. |\n159: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/skill-advisor-brief.ts` | The brief builder: scoring, freshness, status, confidence/uncertainty. |\n160: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/render.ts` | `renderAdvisorBrief` / `renderAdvisorFallbackDirective`, produces the model-visible text. |\n161: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/directive-lifecycle.ts` | Directive-lifecycle dedup: decides whether a directive should be re-delivered to a session within its cadence. |\n162: | `system-skill-advisor/hooks/lib/skill-advisor-cli-fallback.ts` | `buildSkillAdvisorBriefFromCli` is the hook's CLI caller. It runs `skill-advisor.cjs advisor_recommend --json - --no-warm-only` with the hook timeout and sends the request on stdin. The CLI owns daemon startup and local-scorer fallback. The function resolves the CLI, IPC bridge and database paths from one root. It searches `.skilled` first and `.opencode` second. |\n163: | `system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Standalone Python advisor CLI. The advisor CLI runs it as the local scorer when the daemon is unreachable. The hook never calls it directly. |\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 6. CONFIGURATION\n168: \n169: The advisor is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes`, and `on` (case-insensitive) for the shared resolver.\n170: \n171: | Variable | Effect |\n172: |---|---|\n173: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED=1` | Canonical kill-switch. Every adapter checks `isHookEnabled('skill-advisor')` and returns empty/skipped prompt-safe output, and the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstains. |\n174: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. |\n175: | `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Alias. Disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. |\n176: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias that disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. The standalone Python CLI (`skill_advisor.py`) reads only this name, set to exactly `1`, from `runtime/schemas/compat-contract.json`. |\n177: | `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED=1` | Legacy alias. Disables every adapter and makes the `advisor_recommend` direct call abstain. |\n178: | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n179: | `MK_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Older alias of `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`. Master switch that disables this concern along with every other repo hook. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 180]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md\",\"offset\":60,\"limit\":120}"},"time":{"start":1794780774592,"end":1794780775681}}}}
