{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794780856416,"sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","part":{"partID":"prt_1e1467cd60017EahTe5kzCH2eT","sessionID":"ses_e1ebd75cbffeKCKhhfHEFy30Ji","messageID":"msg_1e14631bd0014yocW7DhAyo0m4","type":"tool","id":"call_3008570784314bf095621e7e","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/032-review-pass-five-remediation/scratch/review/report-2.txt; echo \"===== REPORT 3\"; cat specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/032-review-pass-five-remediation/scratch/review/report-3.txt","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"verdict: SHIP\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1052 (Q2) nestedCommand looks for a shell name or `eval` anywhere in the segment with findIndex, not at the command position. So an argument word `eval` turns the rest of the segment into nested text. That drops writes the reader at d0f605b42f8 caught and adds writes to read-only commands. None of these are among the four allowed absences, which the T050c verifier names as the quoted git commit, the quoted tee, tee /dev/null and tee /dev/stderr. The round-two rescore is byte-identical to round one, so no stored run moved. | evidence: probe under mktemp in TMPDIR ran normalize on one Claude Bash call at BASE and FIX. Lost: `mkdir -p eval reports` BASE [\"eval\",\"reports\"] FIX [], `rm -rf eval out.json` BASE [\"eval\",\"out.json\"] FIX [], `touch eval notes.md` BASE [\"eval\",\"notes.md\"] FIX []. Added on read-only: `echo eval rm a.md` BASE [] FIX [\"a.md\"], `printf %s eval touch done.md` BASE [] FIX [\"done.md\"], `grep bash -c 'rm x.md'` BASE [] FIX [\"x.md\"], `bash script.sh -c 'rm x.md'` BASE [] FIX [\"x.md\"] | fix: read `eval` only as the segment's first word after assignments, and a shell name only there or after a known runner prefix (timeout N, env, sudo, nice, xargs), with one lost and one false case in the test table\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1047 (Q2) SHELL_COMMAND_FLAG needs `c` as the last letter, so a cluster with `c` before another letter, which bash accepts, is not read as nested text. BASE caught it through the plain split, with a garbled path. Wrappers outside the shell list (`fish -c`, `ssh host \"...\"`, `watch \"...\"`) lose the same garbled BASE write. That is the same class as the accepted su -c case, so they are noted here and not graded on their own. | evidence: same probe: `bash -cx 'cd x; rm a.md'` BASE [\"a.md'\"] FIX [], `fish -c \"cd x; rm a.md\"` BASE [\"a.md\\\"\"] FIX [], `ssh host \"cd x; rm a.md\"` and `watch \"cd x; rm a.md\"` each BASE [\"a.md\\\"\"] FIX [] | fix: use /^-[A-Za-z]*c[A-Za-z]*$/, and name the wrapper class beside su -c in the accepted risks\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:919 (Q2) shellWord removes every quote and backslash character, not one shell quote layer. A path keeps the wrong characters, and nested text loses its inner quotes before the rescan, so a quoted separator inside nested text splits again. That brings back the first report's quoted git commit false write through `bash -c`. BASE also wrote there, so this is no regression, only an unclosed gap. | evidence: same probe: `bash -c \"git commit -m 'docs; rm stale notes'\"` FIX [\"stale\",\"notes\"], `bash -c \"touch 'a b.md'\"` FIX [\"a\",\"b.md\"], `bash -c 'rm \"a b.md\"'` FIX [\"a\",\"b.md\"], `rm \"it's.md\"` FIX [\"its.md\"] (BASE [\"it's.md\"]), `rm 'a\\b.md'` FIX [\"ab.md\"] (BASE [\"a\\\\b.md\"]) | fix: strip one shell quote layer (single quotes literal, double quotes with backslash escapes, an unquoted backslash dropped) for targets and for the nested text before it is rescanned\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs:287 (Q3) The stale rule now refuses the committed hook edit, which closes the first report's P1. It also widens the over-refusal plan D2 accepted for the dist's own folder to every tracked file under a reached folder. In the real advisor runtime that covers hooks/*.md, hooks/*/README.md, hooks/tests, the .mjs adapters and hooks/pi, which tsconfig.build.json excludes. So a current build is refused after a doc-only commit there, and this commit's own hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md edit is such a case. Plan D2 still states only the own-folder rule. No under-refusal was found: of the six ignored dist trees, only the advisor's tsconfig include climbs out of its folder, and the advisor's imports outside runtime/ and hooks/ come through @spec-kit/shared, which has its own checked dist. | evidence: probe under mktemp in TMPDIR with the real runtime/tsconfig.build.json copied in. Clean and current: `{\"ok\":true,...,\"copied\":[\"skill/runtime/dist\"]}` exit=0. Committed hooks/claude/hook.ts edit: `stale_build` exit=2. Only skill/hooks/README.md newer, hook.ts older than the build: `stale_build` exit=2. A tsconfig scan of every ignored dist printed one climbing include, \"../hooks/**/*.ts\" | fix: count only files matching the include patterns minus exclude (or only *.ts under a reached folder), or record the widened cost in plan D2\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs:554 (Q4) The T045 try now covers the five source checks, but three exits still print no JSON line. headOf at :554 (a source that is not a repository) and mkdirSync at :581 (a destination parent that cannot be written) leave a raw stack, and the usage refusals at :110 to :133 write to stderr only. All three predate this diff. Each exits non-zero and leaves no arena. | evidence: same probe folder. No args: `exit=2 stdout=[] stderr-first=[prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs: --source and --dest are required]`. Source not a repository: `exit=1 stdout=[] stderr-first=[file:///.../prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs:150] stack-lines=7`. Destination under a chmod 555 folder with a fresh build: `exit=1 stdout=[] code=[code: 'EACCES'] stack-lines=6`. `git show d0f605b42f8:<builder>` has headOf at :457 and mkdirSync at :476, both outside a try | fix: wrap the body of main in one try whose catch calls abort, and print a JSON refusal before the usage exits\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:509 (Q1) The first report's Q2 line named `**COMMAND:** /x` as missed at BASE and FIX. It is still missed, and no packet doc records it. The brief records only the indented-code case as left open. A related gap: a fence closes on any run of its character, so a ``` line inside a ```` block ends the block early. That gap predates the diff, because BASE toggled on every ``` line. | evidence: probe ran commandLines on text events at FIX: `boldLabel []`, and a ```` fence holding ``` then a COMMAND line then ```` gave `[{\"command\":\"/deep:review\",\"state\":\"started\"}]`. `grep -rn -i \"bold\\|\\*\\*COMMAND\"` over the phase's .md files printed nothing | fix: allow `**` or `__` after the colon in COMMAND_LINE, or record the case beside the indented-code one, and close a fence only on a run at least as long as its opener\nQ1: closed, apart from the P2 above. P1 stale build: refused (probe above, T044 test). Quoted separators, option values, sed -i with several files and discarded targets: re-run at FIX, every case reads as the fix lines asked. `git commit -m \"docs; rm stale notes\"` [], `echo \"a | tee b\"` [], `mkdir -m 755 dir` [\"dir\"], `touch -r ref.md f.md` [\"f.md\"], `cp -t dest src.md` [\"dest\"], `sed -i 's/a/b/' f1.md f2.md` [\"f1.md\",\"f2.md\"], `mkdir \"my dir\" other` [\"my dir\",\"other\"], `echo x | tee /dev/stderr` [], `touch -d '2 days ago' f.md` [\"f.md\"]. Opening markup `1)`, heading, checkbox and ~~~ fences are read (T051, T051a). Absolute link refused (T046). JSON line for the five source checks (T045). GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file closes SSH (T047). README row and paragraph (T048, T048a). Four MK_ rows in the hook table (T052, skill-advisor-hook.md:179-182). unsafeNoChangedPath and mkdir named in rescore-delta.txt:69-72. | evidence: probe tables c1 and c2 under mktemp in TMPDIR, diff read, `env -u AI_SESSION_CHILD npx vitest run tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts tests/command-behaviour-guards.vitest.ts` printed `Test Files 2 passed (2)` `Tests 373 passed (373)`, exit 0\nQ2: no false write on a read-only command beyond the nestedCommand P2. The sed and perl gate, isShellWriter, is unchanged, so editedWords only picks files for calls BASE already read as writes. The `#` comment case drops `echo hi # don't; rm a.md` (BASE [\"a.md\"]), which the Fable report accepts, because bash runs no rm there. | evidence: probe table c1, 60 commands at BASE and FIX\nQ4: GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file broke nothing found. A local clone still works under it. Pi runs with --offline, which sets PI_OFFLINE, so package installs skip the network, and a failed package does not abort startup. The arena has no remote, so no live turn needs a network transport. Claude, Codex, OpenCode and Devin were graded from reading only. | evidence: `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file git clone -q <src> <dst>` printed `plain-path clone ok exit=0`, and the file:// form also cloned. Pi 1.5.2 dist/main.js:494-496 and dist/core/package-manager.js:890 and :1124 read. Absolute links in the real copied trees: 18 ignored dist and node_modules trees, 110 links, 0 absolute, so T046 does not refuse the real arena\nQ5: no finding. README:111 and :143 state the include rule and the absolute-link refusal as the code does (buildInputFolders, escapingLinks readlinkSync check). README:137 states GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file as childEnv sets it. The four MK_ rows match hook-flags.cjs:41 and :73-75. | evidence: read of the doc diff and of .skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs\nQ6: no finding. 1051 added lines under .skilled hold no spec path, task, finding, phase, lineage, decision or option label, and no em dash. | evidence: `git diff -U0 d0f605b42f8 02261e94254 -- .skilled <three excludes> | grep '^+'` then grep -nE 'specs/|T0[0-9]{2}|REQ-|D(1[0-3]|[1-9])|ADR|P[012]-|iter[0-9]|R[1-4]-P|DM5|verify-5|phase [0-9]|-5b|lineage|Fable|option D|Q[0-9]+|finding' printed nothing, and the em dash count printed 0\nChecks run: the two vitest files above (373 passed). Every probe ran under one mktemp folder in TMPDIR, which was removed afterwards. No prompt row file was opened, hook-flags.env was not read, and no model session, live runner, daemon or build ran.\n===== REPORT 3\nverdict: SHIP\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1104 (Q2) The eight wrapper forms read as no write at FIX. With no separator inside the nested text that matches d0f605b42f8, which read no write either. With a separator inside, BASE caught a garbled write through its plain split and FIX drops it, so the verifier's \"as before the rebuild\" holds only for the single-command form. That is the same class report-2 graded P2 for fish -c, ssh and watch, beside the accepted su -c case, so it is acceptable as a known limit. The limit is recorded only as one clause in the T050e evidence line, which names two of the eight forms, and not in plan D13 or any limits section. Against 02261e94254 this commit drops every one of these forms, which that commit read as writes, but the brief's reference is d0f605b42f8. | evidence: probe under mktemp in TMPDIR ran normalize on one Claude Bash call at d0f605b42f8 (BASE), 02261e94254 (MID) and d21dbc1cafe (FIX). `sudo bash -c 'rm a.md'`, `bash -o pipefail -c 'rm a.md'`, `timeout -k 2 5 bash -c 'rm a.md'`, `bash -O extglob -c`, `bash --rcfile f -c`, `bash +x -c`, `time -p bash -c`, `xargs bash -c`: each BASE [] MID [\"a.md\"] FIX []. `bash -o pipefail -c 'cd x; rm a.md'` and `sudo bash -c 'cd x; rm a.md'`: each BASE [\"a.md'\"] MID [\"x/a.md\"] FIX [] | fix: name the wrapper class (sudo, xargs, a shell option that takes a value, a runner option that takes a value) beside su -c in plan D13\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1062 (Q2) Part of that limit is in the new runner code, not only in shell grammar. The comment says a runner's own words are read before the shell, but afterRunner skips only words that start with `-`, so the value of a value-taking option becomes the \"command\": `timeout -k 2 5` stops at 5, `timeout -s KILL 5` stops at 5, and `env -u FOO` (:1068) stops at FOO. Each then reads no write. BASE read none for these either, so nothing is lost against d0f605b42f8. | evidence: same probe: `timeout -k 2 5 bash -c 'rm a.md'`, `timeout -s KILL 5 bash -c 'rm a.md'`, `env -u FOO bash -c 'rm a.md'` each BASE [] MID [\"a.md\"] FIX [] | fix: consume the value word after timeout -k and -s and after env -u, with one case in the T050e table\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1053 (Q2) A runner word before `eval` adds a false write against d0f605b42f8. `env`, `nohup`, `nice`, `timeout` and `exec` run an external program, and eval is a shell builtin with no file on this system, so the command fails and changes nothing, while FIX reads a write. Only `command` and `time` can run eval. MID read the same writes, so this commit did not add them against MID. Contrived, so P2. | evidence: same probe: `env eval rm a.md`, `nohup eval rm a.md`, `nice eval rm a.md`, `timeout 5 eval rm a.md`, `exec eval rm a.md` each BASE [] FIX [\"a.md\"]. Real shell under the mktemp folder: `ls /usr/bin/eval` printed No such file, `env eval rm a.md` printed `env: eval: No such file or directory` and a.md was still listed | fix: after a runner, accept `eval` only when every runner word was `command` or `time`\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1104 (Q2) The option scan after a shell name does not stop at `--`, so a `-c` after it is read as the command flag. After `--` bash takes `-c` and the next word as positional arguments, so the call changes nothing, while FIX reads a write. MID read the same. Contrived, so P2. | evidence: same probe: `bash -s -- -c 'rm a.md'` BASE [] MID [\"a.md\"] FIX [\"a.md\"]. Real shell: `bash -s -- -c 'rm b.md' </dev/null` left b.md listed | fix: break the option scan on `--`, with one no-write case in the T050e table\nQ1: closed. Every case T050e names reads as asked at FIX. `mkdir -p eval reports` BASE [\"eval\",\"reports\"] MID [] FIX [\"eval\",\"reports\"]. `echo eval rm a.md` BASE [] MID [\"a.md\"] FIX []. `grep bash -c 'rm x.md' notes.md` BASE [] MID [\"x.md\"] FIX []. `bash -cx 'rm a.md'` BASE [] MID [] FIX [\"a.md\"], and a real `bash -cx 'rm c.md'` removed c.md. The rest of the report-2 table also reads right: `rm -rf eval out.json` FIX [\"eval\",\"out.json\"], `touch eval notes.md` FIX [\"eval\",\"notes.md\"], `printf %s eval touch done.md` FIX [], `bash script.sh -c 'rm a.md'` FIX [], `bash -cx 'cd x; rm a.md'` FIX [\"x/a.md\"] (BASE [\"a.md'\"]). The T050c nested cases keep their result: `bash -c 'rm a.md'`, `eval rm a.md`, `timeout 5 bash -lc 'rm a.md'`, `env FOO=1 bash -c`, `nohup sh -c`, `nice -n 5 bash -c` each FIX [\"a.md\"]. | evidence: probe tables c1 and c2 (61 commands) under mktemp in TMPDIR, `git diff 02261e94254 d21dbc1cafe -- .skilled <three excludes>` read, `env -u AI_SESSION_CHILD npx vitest run tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts` printed `Test Files 1 passed (1)` `Tests 330 passed (330)`, exit 0\nQ2: no write d0f605b42f8 caught as a clean path is lost. The only writes lost against BASE are the garbled ones in the wrapper P2 above. The added false writes against BASE are the two contrived P2 lines above (runner before eval, `--` before -c). Every other write FIX adds against BASE is real (`bash -c`, `eval`, runner-led shells, `bash -C -c`, `bash --noediting -c`, `/bin/bash -c 'mkdir eval reports'`). Read-only shapes stay clean: `command -v bash`, `bash -c \"git status\"`, `bash -c 'echo rm a.md'`, `eval echo touch a.md`, `eval \"$(ssh-agent)\"`, `echo hi | bash -c 'cat > out.md'` each FIX []. afterRunner always returns past `at` or -1, so the runner loop ends. Grading the eight wrapper forms: acceptable as a recorded limit at P2, as the first line says. | evidence: probe table c2 under mktemp in TMPDIR\nQ3: no finding. The 90 added lines under .skilled hold no spec path, phase, task, finding, lineage or decision id, and no em dash. | evidence: `git diff -U0 02261e94254 d21dbc1cafe -- .skilled <three excludes> | grep '^+'` then grep -nE 'specs/|T0[0-9]{2}|REQ-|\\bD(1[0-3]|[1-9])\\b|ADR|P[012]-|iter[0-9]|R[1-4]-P|DM5|verify-5|phase [0-9]|-5b|lineage|Fable|option D|\\bQ[0-9]+\\b|finding|[0-9]{3}-[a-z]|SC-|AC-' printed nothing (exit 1), and the em dash count printed 0\nChecks run: the one vitest file above (330 passed). Every probe ran under one mktemp folder in TMPDIR, removed afterwards. No prompt row file was opened (git archive excluded the three), hook-flags.env was not read, and no model session, live runner, daemon or build ran. The worktree's `M goal.md` and `?? review/deep-review-fanout-config-pass-5b.json` in the packet were already present before this review and are not mine.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"verdict: SHIP\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1052 (Q2) nestedCommand looks for a shell name or `eval` anywhere in the segment with findIndex, not at the command position. So an argument word `eval` turns the rest of the segment into nested text. That drops writes the reader at d0f605b42f8 caught and adds writes to read-only commands. None of these are among the four allowed absences, which the T050c verifier names as the quoted git commit, the quoted tee, tee /dev/null and tee /dev/stderr. The round-two rescore is byte-identical to round one, so no stored run moved. | evidence: probe under mktemp in TMPDIR ran normalize on one Claude Bash call at BASE and FIX. Lost: `mkdir -p eval reports` BASE [\"eval\",\"reports\"] FIX [], `rm -rf eval out.json` BASE [\"eval\",\"out.json\"] FIX [], `touch eval notes.md` BASE [\"eval\",\"notes.md\"] FIX []. Added on read-only: `echo eval rm a.md` BASE [] FIX [\"a.md\"], `printf %s eval touch done.md` BASE [] FIX [\"done.md\"], `grep bash -c 'rm x.md'` BASE [] FIX [\"x.md\"], `bash script.sh -c 'rm x.md'` BASE [] FIX [\"x.md\"] | fix: read `eval` only as the segment's first word after assignments, and a shell name only there or after a known runner prefix (timeout N, env, sudo, nice, xargs), with one lost and one false case in the test table\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1047 (Q2) SHELL_COMMAND_FLAG needs `c` as the last letter, so a cluster with `c` before another letter, which bash accepts, is not read as nested text. BASE caught it through the plain split, with a garbled path. Wrappers outside the shell list (`fish -c`, `ssh host \"...\"`, `watch \"...\"`) lose the same garbled BASE write. That is the same class as the accepted su -c case, so they are noted here and not graded on their own. | evidence: same probe: `bash -cx 'cd x; rm a.md'` BASE [\"a.md'\"] FIX [], `fish -c \"cd x; rm a.md\"` BASE [\"a.md\\\"\"] FIX [], `ssh host \"cd x; rm a.md\"` and `watch \"cd x; rm a.md\"` each BASE [\"a.md\\\"\"] FIX [] | fix: use /^-[A-Za-z]*c[A-Za-z]*$/, and name the wrapper class beside su -c in the accepted risks\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:919 (Q2) shellWord removes every quote and backslash character, not one shell quote layer. A path keeps the wrong characters, and nested text loses its inner quotes before the rescan, so a quoted separator inside nested text splits again. That brings back the first report's quoted git commit false write through `bash -c`. BASE also wrote there, so this is no regression, only an unclosed gap. | evidence: same probe: `bash -c \"git commit -m 'docs; rm stale notes'\"` FIX [\"stale\",\"notes\"], `bash -c \"touch 'a b.md'\"` FIX [\"a\",\"b.md\"], `bash -c 'rm \"a b.md\"'` FIX [\"a\",\"b.md\"], `rm \"it's.md\"` FIX [\"its.md\"] (BASE [\"it's.md\"]), `rm 'a\\b.md'` FIX [\"ab.md\"] (BASE [\"a\\\\b.md\"]) | fix: strip one shell quote layer (single quotes literal, double quotes with backslash escapes, an unquoted backslash dropped) for targets and for the nested text before it is rescanned\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs:287 (Q3) The stale rule now refuses the committed hook edit, which closes the first report's P1. It also widens the over-refusal plan D2 accepted for the dist's own folder to every tracked file under a reached folder. In the real advisor runtime that covers hooks/*.md, hooks/*/README.md, hooks/tests, the .mjs adapters and hooks/pi, which tsconfig.build.json excludes. So a current build is refused after a doc-only commit there, and this commit's own hooks/skill-advisor-hook.md edit is such a case. Plan D2 still states only the own-folder rule. No under-refusal was found: of the six ignored dist trees, only the advisor's tsconfig include climbs out of its folder, and the advisor's imports outside runtime/ and hooks/ come through @spec-kit/shared, which has its own checked dist. | evidence: probe under mktemp in TMPDIR with the real runtime/tsconfig.build.json copied in. Clean and current: `{\"ok\":true,...,\"copied\":[\"skill/runtime/dist\"]}` exit=0. Committed hooks/claude/hook.ts edit: `stale_build` exit=2. Only skill/hooks/README.md newer, hook.ts older than the build: `stale_build` exit=2. A tsconfig scan of every ignored dist printed one climbing include, \"../hooks/**/*.ts\" | fix: count only files matching the include patterns minus exclude (or only *.ts under a reached folder), or record the widened cost in plan D2\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs:554 (Q4) The T045 try now covers the five source checks, but three exits still print no JSON line. headOf at :554 (a source that is not a repository) and mkdirSync at :581 (a destination parent that cannot be written) leave a raw stack, and the usage refusals at :110 to :133 write to stderr only. All three predate this diff. Each exits non-zero and leaves no arena. | evidence: same probe folder. No args: `exit=2 stdout=[] stderr-first=[prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs: --source and --dest are required]`. Source not a repository: `exit=1 stdout=[] stderr-first=[file:///.../prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs:150] stack-lines=7`. Destination under a chmod 555 folder with a fresh build: `exit=1 stdout=[] code=[code: 'EACCES'] stack-lines=6`. `git show d0f605b42f8:<builder>` has headOf at :457 and mkdirSync at :476, both outside a try | fix: wrap the body of main in one try whose catch calls abort, and print a JSON refusal before the usage exits\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:509 (Q1) The first report's Q2 line named `**COMMAND:** /x` as missed at BASE and FIX. It is still missed, and no packet doc records it. The brief records only the indented-code case as left open. A related gap: a fence closes on any run of its character, so a ``` line inside a ```` block ends the block early. That gap predates the diff, because BASE toggled on every ``` line. | evidence: probe ran commandLines on text events at FIX: `boldLabel []`, and a ```` fence holding ``` then a COMMAND line then ```` gave `[{\"command\":\"/deep:review\",\"state\":\"started\"}]`. `grep -rn -i \"bold\\|\\*\\*COMMAND\"` over the phase's .md files printed nothing | fix: allow `**` or `__` after the colon in COMMAND_LINE, or record the case beside the indented-code one, and close a fence only on a run at least as long as its opener\nQ1: closed, apart from the P2 above. P1 stale build: refused (probe above, T044 test). Quoted separators, option values, sed -i with several files and discarded targets: re-run at FIX, every case reads as the fix lines asked. `git commit -m \"docs; rm stale notes\"` [], `echo \"a | tee b\"` [], `mkdir -m 755 dir` [\"dir\"], `touch -r ref.md f.md` [\"f.md\"], `cp -t dest src.md` [\"dest\"], `sed -i 's/a/b/' f1.md f2.md` [\"f1.md\",\"f2.md\"], `mkdir \"my dir\" other` [\"my dir\",\"other\"], `echo x | tee /dev/stderr` [], `touch -d '2 days ago' f.md` [\"f.md\"]. Opening markup `1)`, heading, checkbox and ~~~ fences are read (T051, T051a). Absolute link refused (T046). JSON line for the five source checks (T045). GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file closes SSH (T047). README row and paragraph (T048, T048a). Four MK_ rows in the hook table (T052, skill-advisor-hook.md:179-182). unsafeNoChangedPath and mkdir named in rescore-delta.txt:69-72. | evidence: probe tables c1 and c2 under mktemp in TMPDIR, diff read, `env -u AI_SESSION_CHILD npx vitest run tests/command-transcript-score.vitest.ts tests/command-behaviour-guards.vitest.ts` printed `Test Files 2 passed (2)` `Tests 373 passed (373)`, exit 0\nQ2: no false write on a read-only command beyond the nestedCommand P2. The sed and perl gate, isShellWriter, is unchanged, so editedWords only picks files for calls BASE already read as writes. The `#` comment case drops `echo hi # don't; rm a.md` (BASE [\"a.md\"]), which the Fable report accepts, because bash runs no rm there. | evidence: probe table c1, 60 commands at BASE and FIX\nQ4: GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file broke nothing found. A local clone still works under it. Pi runs with --offline, which sets PI_OFFLINE, so package installs skip the network, and a failed package does not abort startup. The arena has no remote, so no live turn needs a network transport. Claude, Codex, OpenCode and Devin were graded from reading only. | evidence: `GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file git clone -q <src> <dst>` printed `plain-path clone ok exit=0`, and the file:// form also cloned. Pi 1.5.2 dist/main.js:494-496 and dist/core/package-manager.js:890 and :1124 read. Absolute links in the real copied trees: 18 ignored dist and node_modules trees, 110 links, 0 absolute, so T046 does not refuse the real arena\nQ5: no finding. README:111 and :143 state the include rule and the absolute-link refusal as the code does (buildInputFolders, escapingLinks readlinkSync check). README:137 states GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=file as childEnv sets it. The four MK_ rows match hook-flags.cjs:41 and :73-75. | evidence: read of the doc diff and of .skilled/hooks/shared/hook-flags.cjs\nQ6: no finding. 1051 added lines under .skilled hold no spec path, task, finding, phase, lineage, decision or option label, and no em dash. | evidence: `git diff -U0 d0f605b42f8 02261e94254 -- .skilled <three excludes> | grep '^+'` then grep -nE 'specs/|T0[0-9]{2}|REQ-|D(1[0-3]|[1-9])|ADR|P[012]-|iter[0-9]|R[1-4]-P|DM5|verify-5|phase [0-9]|-5b|lineage|Fable|option D|Q[0-9]+|finding' printed nothing, and the em dash count printed 0\nChecks run: the two vitest files above (373 passed). Every probe ran under one mktemp folder in TMPDIR, which was removed afterwards. No prompt row file was opened, hook-flags.env was not read, and no model session, live runner, daemon or build ran.\n===== REPORT 3\nverdict: SHIP\nP2 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs:1104 (Q2) The eight wrapper forms read as no write at FIX. With no separator inside the nested text that matches d0f605b42f8, which read no write either. With a separator inside, BASE caught a garbled write through its plain split and FIX drops it, so the verifier